Module-03:-Update
==================================================================

CISCO ASA VPN AUTOMATION – REAL PRODUCTION WORKFLOW

This module handles VPN configuration updates on Cisco ASA.

Workflow Overview:

1.  Netmiko establishes SSH connection to ASA Firewall to read the current configuration and apply

from netmiko import ConnectHandler   #  from Netmiko library we aare importing ConnectHandler class
connect_handler = ConnectHandler(
    device_type="cisco_asa", #(Syntax:parameter=value)
    host="159.66.72.50",, #(Syntax:parameter=value)
    username="admin",, #(Syntax:parameter=value)
    password="password", #(Syntax:parameter=value)
)

2. Disable Pagination

   -Command: terminal pager 0 #To disable pagination

   -Cisco ASA normally pauses long command outputs with "--More--".
    Automation requires full command output without interruption,
    so pagination is disabled.

3. Verify VPN Peer Exists
   Syntax:show run tunnel-group <peer_ip>
   Command:show run tunnel-group 4.227.229.249


4. Identify Crypto Map to check current VPN configuration
   Command:show run crypto map

    The crypto map defines the core VPN configuration including:
    • Crypto map name
    • Sequence number
    • Peer IP
    • Transform set
    • Encryption domain ACL

5. Identify Encryption Domain
-An encryption domain defines which local and remote networks are allowed
 to communicate through the IPsec VPN tunnel.

-Command:show run access-list <acl_name>
-Example:show run access-list outside_cryptomap_1
-Output:
access-list outside_cryptomap_1 extended permit ip
192.168.10.0 255.255.255.0
10.2.5.0 255.255.255.0


6. Parse Object Networks:- To Extract real subnet from object network
-Command:show run

Command
--------------------------------------------------
show run  (to find IP and subnet mask for object network)
--------------------------------------------------
Syntax
--------------------------------------------------
object network <object_name>
 subnet <ip_address> <subnet_mask>

Example Output
--------------------------------------------------
object network 10.2.5.0_24
 subnet 10.2.5.0 255.255.255.0

object network 10.2.6.0_24
 subnet 10.2.6.0 255.255.255.0

How Automation Extracts It
--------------------------------------------------
The automation runs "show run" and reads the output
line-by-line. When it finds the keyword "object network",
it stores the object name. When it finds the "subnet"
line below it, it extracts the IP and subnet mask.

Code with Comment
--------------------------------------------------
show_run = connection.send_command("show run")


objects = {} # dictionary to store object → subnet mapping

current_object = None

for line in show_run.splitlines():

    # detect object network
    if line.startswith("object network"):
        current_object = line.split()[2]

    # detect subnet inside object
    elif line.strip().startswith("subnet") and current_object:

        parts = line.strip().split()

        ip = parts[1]       # subnet IP
        mask = parts[2]     # subnet mask

        objects[current_object] = (ip, mask)

        current_object = None



==================================================================
STEP 7 – Find Object Groups
==================================================================
Object groups allow multiple networks to be grouped
together. The VPN ACL may reference an object group
instead of individual networks.

Automation reads the object group to identify which
object networks are used in the VPN.


Command:show run object-group id <group_name>
--------------------------------------------------


Syntax
--------------------------------------------------
object-group network <group_name>
 network-object object <object_name>

Example
--------------------------------------------------
show run object-group id AZURE_PA_NET

Example Output
--------------------------------------------------
object-group network AZURE_PA_NET
 network-object object 10.2.5.0_24
 network-object object 10.2.6.0_24

==================================================================
Dynamic Object-Group Detection
==================================================================

Why
--------------------------------------------------
In production environments, the object-group name
is not fixed. Different VPN configurations may use
different object-group names.

Example:
AZURE_PA_NET
AWS_REMOTE_NET
REMOTE_SUBNETS

Therefore automation must dynamically identify the
object-group name from the VPN ACL configuration.


Step 1 – Read VPN ACL
--------------------------------------------------
Command

show run access-list <acl_name>

Example

show run access-list outside_cryptomap_1


Example Output

access-list outside_cryptomap_1 extended permit ip
192.168.10.0 255.255.255.0
object-group AZURE_PA_NET


From this ACL line automation detects:

object-group AZURE_PA_NET


Step 2 – Extract Object Group Name
--------------------------------------------------
Python code parses the ACL line and extracts the
object-group name.


Code Example with Comment
--------------------------------------------------

acl_output = connection.send_command(
    "show run access-list outside_cryptomap_1"
)

group_name = None

for line in acl_output.splitlines():

    if "object-group" in line:

        parts = line.split()

        group_name = parts[-1]   # extract object-group name


Result

group_name = AZURE_PA_NET


Step 3 – Run Object Group Command Dynamically
--------------------------------------------------

command = f"show run object-group id {group_name}"

output = connection.send_command(command)


Final Command Executed

show run object-group id AZURE_PA_NET


==================================================================
Summary
==================================================================

1. Automation first finds the ACL used in the VPN
   from the crypto map.

   Command:show run crypto map


2. It reads the ACL to check if an object-group
   is used instead of direct subnets.

   Command:show run access-list <acl_name>


3. If an object-group is found, the automation
   extracts the object-group name dynamically.

   Example:object-group AZURE_PA_NET


4. Then it reads the object-group configuration
   to get all object names.

   Command:
   show run object-group id <group_name>


5. Finally, it reads the object network definitions
   from the running configuration to convert object
   names into real subnet values.

   Command:
   show run


Result:Automation identifies the actual remote subnets
used in the VPN configuration.
Object-group name is NOT hardcoded.

Automation flow:

show run crypto map
        │
        ▼
Find ACL name
        │
        ▼
show run access-list <ACL>
        │
        ▼
Detect object-group
        │
        ▼
show run object-group id <group_name>
        │
        ▼
Get object names
        │
        ▼
show run
        │
        ▼
Get real subnet
